\section{Specify the Problem Before Searching}

A superoptimizer takes a small computation and searches for a cheaper program with the same observable behavior. Specify the live inputs and outputs, preconditions, allowed operations, and metric before using the word ``cheaper.'' A minimum instruction count does not necessarily minimize bytes, and neither minimizes cycles on every core. Equivalence depends on which flags survive, whether inputs may alias, and whether the replacement may fault on inputs where the reference is defined. In a pure, fixed-width IR fragment the observation might be one bit vector. In a machine fragment it can include a vector of live registers, flags, and memory effects.

Henry Massalin's \emph{Superoptimizer---A Look at the Smallest Program} searches a chosen instruction subset, using fast probabilistic tests to discard wrong candidates. Enumeration by length gives a meaningful minimality guarantee only if the allocation of operands, immediate domain, instruction subset, and bound are all stated and the smaller spaces have been exhausted. Testing is useful because almost all candidates are wrong, not because passing a suite makes a candidate universally correct.

\section{Three Ways to Generate Candidates}

Exhaustive search can canonicalize temporary names and, where semantics permit, order commuting operands. A solver-aided alternative encodes choices of operations, constants, and wiring. Sasnauskas and colleagues' \emph{A Synthesizing Superoptimizer} describes Souper's use of counterexample-guided inductive synthesis on fragments of LLVM IR. Synthesize against a finite sample, then ask whether any allowed input distinguishes the proposal. A satisfying answer supplies another sample; an unsatisfiable answer validates the candidate under the encoded semantics. A timeout establishes neither correctness nor impossibility. For a language with undefined behavior, the check needs the appropriate refinement relation; it is not in general enough to compare two total functions that silently give arbitrary values to undefined inputs.

Schkufza, Sharma, and Aiken's \emph{Stochastic Superoptimization} instead explores loop-free binary programs by sampling mutations and ranking by a cost that includes both correctness and performance terms. The sampler can visit incorrect intermediate programs and move between otherwise separated parts of the search space. The test-derived correctness term is a search heuristic; promising candidates still go to verification. These search modes have different failure reports: exhaustive search can rule out a bounded space, solver-aided search may return unknown, and sampling can end without establishing anything about all the candidates it did not visit.

\section{Worked Rewrite: Multiply by Nine}

Consider 64-bit unsigned wrapping arithmetic. A baseline machine fragment, in Intel syntax, is
\begin{verbatim}
mov rax, rdi
shl rax, 3
add rax, rdi
\end{verbatim}
One candidate is \verb|lea rax, [rdi + rdi*8]|. Intel's \emph{Instruction Set Reference} specifies that this effective address calculation does not read memory or change the arithmetic flags. For a test restricted to the value in \texttt{rax}, the counterexample query is
\[
  \exists x:\mathrm{BV}_{64}.\quad
  ((x\ll 3)+x)\ne (x+8x),
\]
where both additions and the multiplication are modulo $2^{64}$. It is unsatisfiable by the semantics of the shift. Include $x=0$, $x=2^{63}$, and $x=2^{64}-1$ in tests of the instruction model; these exercise zero and discarded high bits that tests on small positive values do not.

Now change the contract so that the zero flag is live. With $x=0$ and an incoming zero flag of zero, the baseline sets it and the candidate leaves it at zero. A validator needs no new search heuristic to reject the substitution; it only needs the right observation. Similarly, the syntax of the \texttt{lea} operand must not lead a model to treat it as a dereference. That would add spurious faults at inputs whose arithmetic result is not a usable pointer.

