\section{Use Counterexamples to Strengthen the Sample}

A synthesis loop need not ask the verifier about every
syntactically possible program. Fix a finite library of pure
straight-line candidates over eight-bit unsigned words and
specify one returned word as the entire observation. The
candidate generator can enumerate programs in increasing
cost, retaining only ones agreeing with the current sample.
Use an independent exhaustive check over all $256$ inputs
as the final verifier for this toy problem. A search budget
may stop the process, but budget exhaustion does not certify
the current proposal.

\begin{sudocode}
\Require Reference $F$, ordered candidate library $C$, sample $S$
\ForAll{$f\in C$ in cost order}
  \To{The sample is a filter, never a proof.}
  \If{$f$ agrees with $F$ on every input in $S$}
    \Let{r}{\operatorname{firstDisagreement}(f,F,\{0,\ldots,255\})}
    \If{$r$ is absent after full enumeration}
      \State \Return verified $f$
    \EndIf
    \To{Every later proposal will face this input.}
    \State Add $r$ to $S$
  \EndIf
\EndFor
\State \Return no match in this library
\end{sudocode}

Take $F(x)=3x\bmod 256$ and start with sample $\{0\}$.
A library containing just identity followed by
$((x\ll1)+x)\bmod 256$ first proposes the identity.
Exhaustive checking returns the least disagreement $1$:
the proposal gives $1$ and the reference gives $3$.
The second candidate passes the sample and the full
check. At $x=128$ the shift has low word $0$ and
the candidate returns $128$, which agrees with
$384\bmod256$. Repeat with a library containing only
identity and expect no match, not a proof that the
reference cannot be optimized.

Every sample entry has a well-defined reference result;
rejecting a candidate for any entry cannot exclude a
universal match. If the whole ordered library is
searched, a returned match is cheapest only within that
library and its cost ordering, not among all programs.
For $w$ input bits, the direct verifier has exponential
input count; a larger width may need a bit-vector proof
instead. Move flags, traps, and memory into the observation
before adapting the checker to any instruction that can affect them.

\section{Make Discovery Reusable}

For offline rule discovery, store the target features, live-out mask, preconditions, objective, and validation result with each discovered rule. A matcher must discharge the preconditions at every application site; the example's flag mask cannot be forgotten once a pretty pattern has been found. Measure the effect after scheduling and allocation as well: a weighted instruction count chosen during search is not a measurement. In an online tool, cap search and proof time and retain the known-good baseline. The dossier's \href{https://github.com/Chubek/TheTomeOfLangDev/blob/HEAD/dossier-on-modern-cc-design/chapters/32-counterexample-guided-compiler-synthesis.md}{Counterexample-Guided Compiler Synthesis} treats these boundaries as part of a compiler architecture.
